SUMMARY:Old Twelfth Night Wassail
DESCRIPTION:History: When Britain adopted the Gregorian calendar in 1752\, 
 many West Country farmers kept the old January date for Twelfth Night to bl
 ess orchards against blight.\nTraditions: Wassailers bang pans\, fire muske
 ts into branches\, and sing to wake the apple trees while offering cider-so
 aked toast to the guardian robin.\nFeasting: Hot mulled cider\, apple cakes
 \, blue cheese\, and pork pies fuel the rounds from farm to farm through th
 e frosty fields.\nDay: Saturday\n\nIcon: 🌟\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nCategory: cultur
 al\\\\\\
CATEGORIES:cultural\\\\\\
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:https://dcmcshan.github.io/MaybeSomethingSeasonal/images/old-twelfth-night-traditional-date-before-calendar-reform.png
END:VEVENT
